package cajeta.gpu;

/**
 * Memory residency of a {@link GpuBuffer} — where the device storage physically
 * lives and how the host and device share it. Selected once at construction
 * (`heap GpuBuffer<float32>(n, MemoryKind.Unified)`); the default constructor uses
 * {@link #Device}, preserving the original explicit-transfer model.
 *
 * This mirrors {@link TextureFormat}: a single clean axis whose **ordinal is the
 * stable native contract** — the `__cajeta_xpu_buffer_*` intrinsics (see
 * [`runtime/native/cajeta_runtime.c`](../../../native/cajeta_runtime.c)) switch
 * on it to pick the per-backend allocator (`hipMalloc` vs `hipMallocManaged` vs
 * `hipHostMalloc`; the CUDA analogues) and the matching deallocator. Do **not**
 * reorder without updating that C side.
 *
 * The three kinds:
 *   - {@link #Device} — device-local memory; the host moves data in/out with the
 *     explicit `upload`/`download` transfers. The default and, on a discrete
 *     GPU, the fastest for device-resident compute.
 *   - {@link #Pinned} — page-locked, device-accessible host memory (CUDA/HIP
 *     "host alloc"). Faster, asynchronous DMA than pageable host memory, and the
 *     kernel can read it directly via the unified address space. Use {@link
 *     GpuBuffer#hostStore}/{@link GpuBuffer#hostLoad} for zero-copy host access.
 *   - {@link #Unified} — managed memory backed by one pointer the host AND device
 *     both address (CUDA/HIP "managed alloc"). On an integrated GPU/APU (e.g.
 *     Strix Halo, where CPU and GPU share physical RAM) this is genuine
 *     zero-copy: no transfer, no migration. `hostStore`/`hostLoad` are plain
 *     memcpys in the shared address space.
 *
 * BACKENDS. CUDA and HIP honour all three kinds. On Vulkan every buffer is
 * already host-visible + coherent (effectively unified on this integrated
 * device), so the kind collapses to that one path. On the CPU "device" memory IS
 * host memory, so every kind is host-accessible. Where a backend or driver lacks
 * the managed/pinned entry point, the allocation falls back to plain device
 * memory — correct, just without the zero-copy property.
 */
public enum MemoryKind {
    Device,    // 0 — device-local; explicit upload/download (default)
    Pinned,    // 1 — page-locked, device-accessible host memory (fast DMA)
    Unified    // 2 — managed memory shared by host + device (zero-copy on an APU)
}
